Read inputs/Q.tex and inputs/P.tex. The scan that selected this pair judged
it from the abstracts alone, feasibility 65 and gain 55
out of 100, and named this connexion: Q's mechanism-design proposals (peer scoring for discipline, reward coupling to induce competition, and a formal sandbagging model of concealed capability) could be implemented as incentive interventions inside P's open-source Energy Society testbed and tested against its existing baseline of self-serving/competitive LLM agent behavior. Its reasoning:
Feasibility is moderately high because P already provides a working multi-agent LLM simulation with configurable competitive/cooperative incentive structures and released code, so adding a specific mechanism from Q (e.g. coupled rewards or peer-scoring) is an engineering extension rather than a new build, though designing a faithful implementation of Q's formal mechanism and running enough trials for a clean comparison is real effort. Gain is moderate: P's survival-pressure setup already surfaces exactly the phenomena Q's applications target (self-serving behavior under competition, incentive-driven concealment resembling sandbagging), so testing whether real LLM agents respond to a theoretically motivated mechanism as predicted would be a genuine theory-practice bridge, but the connexion is still one of several plausible testbeds for Q's ideas rather than uniquely enabled by P, which caps how decisive the result could be. Treat that as a first hypothesis, not an instruction; confirm
it, sharpen it or replace it. Then look for an advance that neither
paper makes alone: a result one supplies that the other can use, a claim
one makes that the other tests or breaks, a construction that combines
them, a question that only becomes visible with both in view. Prefer one
concrete, supported line over several vague ones. Sharpen the question if
that helps, and say when you have changed it. No quota of ideas and no
forced positive result; "nothing here" with reasons is a valid finding.

